From cf9044244750c8df9c378aed05d35f57a1736c00 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: agent-keys <noreply@agent-keys.invalid>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 21:08:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/11] fix(operator): the Safe's standing grant is the owner's
 -- signer-migrate reads it, never sends it

The migration's first step, `kernel-approves-safe`, sent a lone
`IdentityRegistry.setApprovalForAll(Safe, true)` through agent-signer with the
agent's permission. AgentPolicy treats an operator grant like an allowance:
one the agent opens has to be withdrawn before the same operation ends, so the
operation is refused and `jinn signer-migrate` could not complete (the internal
audit of 2026-11-28, finding AK-2026-11-28-03). The hermetic loop did not show
it: its bytecode fixture carried an earlier AgentPolicy.

- signer/migrate.ts: the step is `owner-approved-safe`, a pure check of
  `isApprovedForAll(Kernel, Safe)`. Missing, in a dry run too, the command
  changes nothing and prints the owner's command (`agent-signer op send
  --root --target <IdentityRegistry> --data <setApprovalForAll(Safe, true)>`,
  with the account's own agent-signer arguments from the CLI verb). The grant
  is never sent with the agent's permission, and the mandate needs no
  `setApprovalForAll` entry.
- The old step was also the proof that the Kernel's key signs under its
  mandate before the Safe changes hands. That proof is now the attestation-key
  publication (`setMetadata`, a call the mandate already lists): the order is
  agent NFT, attestation key, Safe, and while the Safe is still to be handed
  over the Kernel publishes whatever the registry already says
  (ensureAttestationKeyBinding `always`). If it cannot sign, the Safe stays
  with the EOA.
- tests: unit (no grant: nothing sent, the exact command; the order of the
  three transactions; no swap when the Kernel does not sign; it signs again
  when the key is already published). On the snapshot's IdentityRegistry,
  signer-migrate-refusals.test.ts: without the grant nothing changes.
- full-loop-kernel-owner.test.ts: the owner's grant is a root operation of the
  test (the owner key of TPM_SIGN_KEYS is opened for it), the mandate lists no
  `setApprovalForAll`, the expected steps follow the new order. Run with an
  agent-signer built with software keys on the fixture below: it passes.
- test/_support/fixtures/tpm-sign-aa-code.json: P256Validator, P256Signer,
  AgentPolicy and SafeNonceAssert are agent-keys' build of the sources of its
  commit 54f1619cb244fbcab924b8bc2f9bd9b098e641d7 (`forge build --evm-version
  osaka`), the AgentPolicy that refuses a paymaster field naming no paymaster,
  rewritten by its examples/jinn/aa-fixture.py.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
